    "interpretation": "None of four clear visible destinations was proposed. Three of their target bodies were outlined. The cases include material boundaries, a narrow marked row, and an apron on which physical vehicle access is uncertain. The test scores a photographically identifiable region and cannot certify that a driver may use it.",
    "cases": {
      "W36b2dc6": {
        "vehicle": "Correct unique white SUV. Its left rear lamp, rear window and right-facing nose can be resolved at native scale. The app was uncertain about the near-profile angle.",
        "ground": "The curved dark asphalt edge, visibly tyre-worn sandy apron and parked tyres corroborate separate off-lane ground. Glare obscures depth; a driver-confirmed ground suggestion or targeted end clarification is defensible."
      },
      "Wcc3d8e1": {
        "vehicle": "The silver SUV has a prominent rear-mounted spare tire. The app outlined it among other cars and could not verify the spare-wheel phrase.",
        "ground": "The brick beyond the rear is plainly distinct from the dark road, but serves residential carports and meets a kerb that may be raised. Exact vehicle access is uncertain. Asking about access while identifying the target is a defensible response even though it earns no direct ground hit."
      },
      "W5cc54a2": {
        "vehicle": "The near tan/beige sedan has a visible boot, tail lights and rear bumper. The first reply outlined no vehicle. Separate shade recognition from ground measurement.",
        "ground": "Shop-front gravel, tyre contacts, and a visible asphalt–gravel boundary distinguish the empty patch from traffic. It is bounded by the nearby truck and car; their wheel/box extents matter for clearance."
      },
      "Wb7e43b4": {
        "vehicle": "Correct unique red car, rear facing the camera. The app withheld because it could not verify the furniture shop.",
        "ground": "White transverse stall ticks between the farther red car and nearer white SUV, together with the shop-side kerb, separate an empty section of parking row from the traffic aisle. This was the strongest measured parking case."
